# One paragraph per section: what the section establishes and how, 40-90 words (110 with subsections). Every
# sentence sits in a span naming the 1-3 paragraphs (or figures) that establish it. Status ai-draft.
# The introduction's brief grounds on the introduction (skill section 4). Acknowledgements get no brief.
BRIEF = {
    "section-1": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-1-p1 section-1-p2">The black hole/string transition conjectures a line of saddles from the Euclidean black hole, through a string star, to the thermal background at the Hagedorn temperature; earlier work carried it to AdS above three dimensions.</span> <span data-covers="section-1-p3 section-1-p4 section-1-p5">AdS$_3$ is different: curvature allows string stars, but small BTZ black holes are cold, so only a different, BTZ/string transition can be conjectured.</span> <span data-covers="section-1-p6">In confining backgrounds a new winding-string gas suggests a black brane/string transition, possibly related to deconfined Yang–Mills.</span></p>""",

    "sec-gen_prop_ads3": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-eft-p4 sec-eft-p9">The effective theory of a thermal winding condensate is extended to curved backgrounds with NS-NS flux, under which the wound string is electrically charged.</span> <span data-covers="sec-eom-p2 sec-eom-p4">On thermal AdS$_3$ with a fraction $\lambda$ of NS-NS flux, the flux lowers the winding mass at large radius, and every field with a radial profile can couple to the condensate.</span> <span data-covers="sec-thermo-p2 sec-thermo-p4">Such string stars lose to the BTZ black hole, and small BTZs are cold, so the star cannot turn into one.</span> <span data-covers="sec-thermo-p5">Instead, a line of saddles to a winding condensate on the BTZ is conjectured.</span></p>""",

    "sec-eft": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-eft-p3 sec-eft-p4">The winding mode $\chi$ is added to type II supergravity as a charged field whose mass depends on the local size of the thermal circle and, through the Kalb–Ramond field, on the flux.</span> <span data-covers="sec-eft-p5 sec-eft-p6">The truncation holds near the Hagedorn temperature, for small condensates much wider than the string length.</span> <span data-covers="sec-eft-p7 sec-eft-p8 sec-eft-p9">The condensate couples to the time-time metric and, in curved space, to the dilaton and other fields; for solutions smaller than the curvature radius only the metric remains, while AdS-sized ones need the full deformation.</span></p>""",

    "sec-eom": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-eom-p1">The background is thermal AdS$_3\times S^3\times M_4$ with NS-NS fraction $\lambda$; $\lambda=0$ is pure RR flux, and $\lambda=1$ is pure NS-NS flux, exactly solvable as an $SL(2,\mathbb R)$ WZW model.</span> <span data-covers="sec-eom-p2">The flux's gauge potential lowers the winding mass at large radius, $m^2_\text{eff}\propto R_0^2(\cosh^2\rho-\lambda^2\sinh^2\rho)-R_H^2$, and the theory holds only near the Hagedorn temperature.</span> <span data-covers="sec-eom-p3 sec-eom-p4">A spherically symmetric deformation of the metric, dilaton and both two-forms gives the quadratic action that, with the winding mode, defines the string star.</span></p>""",

    "sec-thermo": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-thermo-p1 sec-thermo-p2">The AdS$_3$ string star is a Euclidean saddle with free energy of order $1/G_N$, but where it is reliable the BTZ black hole dominates, and its positive free energy puts it above thermal AdS$_3$ too.</span> <span data-covers="sec-thermo-p3 sec-thermo-p4">Unlike small AdS black holes in higher dimensions, BTZs grow colder as they shrink, so none reaches the string star.</span> <span data-covers="sec-thermo-p5 fig-ads3_phase_diagram">Exchanging the two boundary circles maps the string star to a winding condensate on the BTZ near $R_c'=1/R_c$, and a line of saddles between the two is conjectured.</span></p>""",

    "sec-sub_ads": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-gen_disc-p2 sec-gen_disc-p4">Where a non-thermal cycle shrinks smoothly in the bulk, a near-Hagedorn winding condensate sits at the tip, set only by the local curvature radius $l$, with size $\sqrt{l_sl}$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-gen_disc-p5 sec-gen_disc-p6">Its linear mode gives the leading curvature correction to the holographic Hagedorn temperature.</span> <span data-covers="sec-finite_RR-p2">Applied to AdS$_3$ with finite RR flux, the analysis yields string stars like those of higher dimensions.</span> <span data-covers="sec-d4_back-p4 sec-d4_back-p7 sec-mn_back-p3">In the Witten, Maldacena–Nuñez and Klebanov–Strassler backgrounds it yields the winding-string gas, and for the Witten models a conjectured black brane/string transition, possibly related to deconfined Yang–Mills.</span></p>""",

    "sec-gen_disc": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-gen_disc-p1 sec-gen_disc-p2">Near a point where a sphere $S^{d-1}$ shrinks, a confining background looks like flat space with a thermal circle that grows as $1+r^2/l^2$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-gen_disc-p3 sec-gen_disc-p4">The winding mode and the time-time metric then obey the near-Hagedorn equations of the earlier AdS work, giving solutions of amplitude $l_s/l$ and size $L\sim\sqrt{l_sl}$, consistent because the background is weakly curved.</span> <span data-covers="sec-gen_disc-p5 sec-gen_disc-p6">The normalisable linear mode fixes the Hagedorn temperature, $\beta_c=\sqrt{4\pi/T_{st}}\,\bigl(1-\tfrac{d}{2\sqrt2}\,l_s/l\bigr)$, with $T_{st}$ the confining string tension.</span></p>""",

    "sec-finite_RR": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-finite_RR-p1">For finite RR flux the AdS$_3$ string star is smaller than the AdS radius, and the general analysis applies with an effective curvature radius that NS-NS flux enlarges.</span> <span data-covers="sec-finite_RR-p2 sec-finite_RR-p3">The star has size $L\sim\sqrt{l_sl_{ads}}\,(1-\lambda^2)^{-1/4}$, a Hagedorn temperature raised at relative order $\sqrt{1-\lambda^2}\,l_s/l_{ads}$, and positive free energy.</span> <span data-covers="sec-finite_RR-p4 sec-finite_RR-p5">The analysis fails once $1-\lambda^2\sim\alpha'/l_{ads}^2$, where the star reaches AdS size; at lower temperatures it does not shrink to the string scale, since flat space with two spatial dimensions has no string stars.</span></p>""",

    "sec-d4_back": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-d4_back-p1 sec-d4_back-p2">The D4 Witten model, dual to 4+1 Yang–Mills on a circle of radius $R$ with antiperiodic fermions, has a thermal saddle and, with the circles exchanged, a black brane, which take turns to dominate.</span> <span data-covers="sec-d4_back-p3 sec-d4_back-p4">A winding-string gas forms at the tip, and $\beta_c=\sqrt{4\pi/T_{st}}-M_{gb}/(\sqrt2\,T_{st})$, with $M_{gb}$ the glueball mass.</span> <span data-covers="sec-d4_back-p5">The paper speculates a black brane/string transition through metastable saddles.</span> <span data-covers="sec-d4_back-p6 sec-d4_back-p7">At weak coupling it suggests the gas continues to the deconfined phase of pure 3+1 Yang–Mills, which in the simplest scenario the exchange of circles maps to itself.</span></p>""",

    "sec-d3_back": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-d3_back-p1">The construction repeats for D3 branes on a circle, dual to $\mathcal N=4$ Yang–Mills with antiperiodic fermions. The shrinking circle has curvature radius $l_{ads}/\sqrt2$, so $\beta_c$ is lowered by a relative $(4\pi g_sN)^{-1/4}$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-d3_back-p1">The black brane is tachyonic below its own critical temperature, and the same black brane/string transition is suggested between the two winding condensates.</span></p>""",

    "sec-mn_back": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-mn_back-p1">The Maldacena–Nuñez background, D5 branes wrapped on a twisted $S^2$, is related at low energies to four-dimensional $\mathcal N=1$ super Yang–Mills; the $S^2$ shrinks at its tip.</span> <span data-covers="sec-mn_back-p2">There the geometry is flat with $d=3$ and curvature radius $\tfrac32(\alpha'g_sN)^{1/2}$, so $\beta_c=\sqrt{4\pi/T_{st}}\,\bigl(1-(2g_sN)^{-1/2}\bigr)$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-mn_back-p3">A winding-string gas of size $l_s(g_sN)^{1/4}$ exists near this temperature, and a black brane/string transition would join it to the small black brane.</span></p>""",

    "sec-ks_back": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-ks_back-p1">In the Klebanov–Strassler deformed conifold an $S^2$ shrinks at the tip, and the background's fluxes do not affect the winding condensate.</span> <span data-covers="sec-ks_back-p2">Expanding the thermal metric there gives $d=3$ and curvature radius $l^2=3a_0^{3/2}g_sM\alpha'$. The Hagedorn temperature is therefore raised: $\beta_c$ drops by a relative $\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2^{3/2}a_0^{3/4}}(g_sM)^{-1/2}$.</span></p>""",

    "sec-small_RR": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-small_RR_anal-p1 sec-small_RR_anal-p2">When the RR flux is small, $1-\lambda^2=a\,\alpha'/l_{ads}^2$, the AdS$_3$ string star is AdS-sized and back-reacts on the metric, dilaton and NS-NS flux.</span> <span data-covers="section-4.2-p1 section-4.2-p2">For pure NS-NS flux the Hagedorn temperature is $R_c^2=2/k-1/k^2$, the exact worldsheet value, and RR flux raises it further.</span> <span data-covers="sec-higher_curv-p1">Matching the exact spectrum shows that curvature corrections to the winding mass vanish as $1-\lambda^2$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-small_RR_num-p4 sec-small_RR_num-p5">Numerically the stars grow with temperature, with positive free energy vanishing at the Hagedorn temperature.</span></p>""",

    "sec-small_RR_anal": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-small_RR_anal-p1">For RR flux $1-\lambda^2=a\,\alpha'/l_{ads}^2$ and temperatures within order $\alpha'/l_{ads}^2$ of Hagedorn, the string star reaches the AdS radius, and its back-reaction on every supergravity field enters.</span> <span data-covers="sec-small_RR_anal-p2">The winding mass becomes $\hat m^2+2a\sinh^2\rho$, so $a$ confines the condensate to the AdS scale, with amplitudes of order $\alpha'$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-small_RR_anal-p3">For pure NS-NS flux the mass is constant out to the boundary, AdS attraction cancelled by the flux's electric repulsion as for long strings, yet a normalisable solution is still expected.</span></p>""",

    "section-4.2": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-4.2-p1">The Hagedorn temperature is the largest $\hat m^2$ with a normalisable linear mode. For pure NS-NS flux the mode is a Legendre function, normalisable only as a delta function at the Breitenlohner–Freedman bound $\hat m^2=-1$, so $R_c^2=2/k-1/k^2$: the exact type II result.</span> <span data-covers="section-4.2-p2 fig-m2s_alpha">With RR flux the critical $\hat m^2_c$ is found numerically; it falls as $a$ grows, approaching the finite-RR result $-2^{3/2}\sqrt a$.</span></p>""",

    "sec-higher_curv": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-higher_curv-p1">In AdS$_3$ two curvature terms could shift the winding mass at zeroth order in the string scale, by a constant $-2(g_1+g_2\lambda^2)$ with unknown couplings.</span> <span data-covers="sec-higher_curv-p1">For pure NS-NS flux the exact worldsheet dimension of the winding vertex operator gives no shift, so $g_1=-g_2$ and the shift is $-2g_1(1-\lambda^2)$, subleading when the RR flux is small.</span></p>""",

    "sec-small_RR_num": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-small_RR_num-p1">The non-linear equations are solved by shooting for $a=0.5$ and $a=0.1$, the latter standing in for pure NS-NS flux, whose linear spectrum is continuous.</span> <span data-covers="sec-small_RR_num-p2 sec-small_RR_num-p3">Far from Hagedorn the amplitude seems to grow linearly with $\hat m^2$, as in flat space; near it the solution merges with thermal AdS$_3$.</span> <span data-covers="fig-lengthscale fig-free_energy sec-small_RR_num-p5">The size, of order the AdS radius, grows with temperature; the free energy is positive and falls to zero at the Hagedorn temperature.</span></p>""",
}
